-- title:
--   Generic Noether normalisation over a domain
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is a domain, let $C$ be a commutative ring which is a domain, and let $C$ be an $R$-algebra whose scalar action is faithful (so the structure map $R \to C$ is injective) and of finite type, i.e. $C$ is generated as an $R$-algebra by finitely many elements. The assertion is that there exist an element $r \in R$ with $r \neq 0$, a natural number $d$, and a family $z : \mathrm{Fin}\ d \to C$ of elements of $C$ that is algebraically independent over $R$ (the induced $R$-algebra map from the polynomial ring in $d$ variables is injective), such that for every $c \in C$ there is a natural number $n$ for which $r^{n} \cdot c$ is integral over the $R$-subalgebra $R[z_{0},\dots,z_{d-1}]$ of $C$ generated by the range of $z$, that is, $r^{n} \cdot c$ satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in that subalgebra. Note that $d$ is not asserted to be the transcendence degree of $\mathrm{Frac}(C)$ over $\mathrm{Frac}(R)$, and no finiteness of $C$ over the subalgebra is claimed; only the stated pointwise integrality after multiplication by a power of the single element $r$.
--
--   This is the relative, or "generic", form of Noether's normalisation lemma: after inverting one nonzero element of the base domain, a finitely generated domain over $R$ becomes integral over a polynomial subalgebra. It is used in this development to control transcendence degrees of quotients and fibres, being cited by [`Algebra.FiniteType.exists_notMem_under_forall_trdeg_quotient_le`](thm.html#Algebra.FiniteType.exists_notMem_under_forall_trdeg_quotient_le), [`Algebra.TensorProduct.trdeg_quotient_le_and_mem_minimalPrimes_iff_of_mem_minimalPrimes`](thm.html#Algebra.TensorProduct.trdeg_quotient_le_and_mem_minimalPrimes_iff_of_mem_minimalPrimes) and [`Algebra.exists_monic_irreducible_map_algebraicClosure_hypersurfaceModel_of_forall_isSeparable`](thm.html#Algebra.exists_monic_irreducible_map_algebraicClosure_hypersurfaceModel_of_forall_isSeparable).
